2026-10-09 18:25:35.554 DEBUG icod.agent.runtime.loop: STEP 1: model responded ->\n  - text: Good context. That changes the calculus significantly. Here's how I'd think about it:\n\n## The Real Constraint\n\nYour demo needs to say: *"We understand formula engines deeply enough to build one, and this architecture scales to the full 400+ function set."* You're not just demoing a feature — you're demoing **architectural competence**.\n\nThat rules out dropping in HyperFormula or formulajs as a black box. A client evaluating a team for a complex product wants to see that you understand the internals, not that you can `npm install`.\n\n## Recommended Approach: Custom Engine, Column-Level Formulas\n\n### Why column-level (not cell-level)\n\nYour data model is row-as-record (JSON values keyed by column ID), not a traditional cell grid. Cell-level references like `A1`, `B3` don't make sense here — Smartsheet doesn't use them either. Instead:\n\n```\nColumn "Total" has formula: =[Price] * [Quantity]\nColumn "Status" has formula: =IF([Total] > 1000, "High", "Low")\nColumn "Summary" has formula: =CONCATENATE([Name], " - ", [Status])\n```\n\nEach formula lives on the **Column** and evaluates per-row against that row's values. Cross-row aggregations use functions:\n\n```\nColumn "Avg Price" has formula: =AVERAGE([Price])   // aggregates all rows\n```\n\n### Architecture (4 layers, all custom)\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│  Formula Bar UI (frontend)              │  ← user types formula\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Lexer → Parser → AST                   │  ← pure JS, ~400 lines\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Evaluator + Function Registry          │  ← walks AST, calls functions\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│  Dependency Tracker + Recalc Engine     │  ← invalidates dependents\n└─────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n**Layer 1 — Lexer**: Tokenizes `=IF([Price] > 100, [Price] * 0.9, [Price])` into tokens: `IF`, `(`, `[Price]`, `>`, `100`, `,`, etc.\n\n**Layer 2 — Parser**: Recursive-descent parser builds an AST:\n```js\n{\n  type: "FunctionCall",\n  name: "IF",\n  args: [\n    { type: "BinaryOp", op: ">", left: { type: "ColumnRef", name: "Price" }, right: { type: "Number", value: 100 } },\n    { type: "BinaryOp", op: "*", left: { type: "ColumnRef", name: "Price" }, right: { type: "Number", value: 0.9 } },\n    { type: "ColumnRef", name: "Price" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n**Layer 3 — Evaluator + Function Registry**: Walks the AST, resolves `[Price]` to the current row's value, calls registered functions. Adding a new function is a one-liner:\n\n```js\nregistry.register("SUM", (...args) => args.flat().reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0));\nregistry.register("IF", (cond, a, b) => cond ? a : b);\n```\n\n**Layer 4 — Dependency Tracker**: When Column A's formula references `[B]`, record that dependency. When column B's values change, mark A as dirty and re-evaluate. Simple adjacency list + topological sort.\n\n### Where evaluation happens\n\n| Context | Where | Why |\n|---------|-------|-----|\n| User typing in formula bar | **Client** | Instant preview, no round-trip |\n| Row data loaded/rendered | **Client** | Compute formula column values on the fly |\n| Row saved to server | **Server validates** | Catch circular refs, invalid formulas |\n\nComputed values are **not stored** — they're derived on read. The formula string is the source of truth. This avoids the stale-data problem entirely.\n\n### Function set (demo scope)\n\nStart with ~25 functions across categories:\n\n| Category | Functions |\n|----------|-----------|\n| Math | SUM, AVERAGE, MIN, MAX, ROUND, ABS, COUNT |\n| Logical | IF, AND, OR, NOT |\n| Text | CONCATENATE, UPPER, LOWER, TRIM, LEN, LEFT, RIGHT |\n| Comparison | built into operators: `=`, `<>`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=` |\n| Lookup | COUNTIF, SUMIF |\n| Date | TODAY, NOW, DATEDIF |\n\nThe registry pattern makes adding the remaining 375+ functions mechanical — each is just a JS function with a name and arity check.\n\n### Schema change\n\nMinimal — one field on Column:\n\n```prisma\nmodel Column {\n  // ... existing fields\n  formula  String?   // ... (+1037 chars)
